\description{
Fits a mixed-effects model: the random effects that \code{\link[=insert]{insert()}} with effect
formulas put into the parameter transformation are integrated out, and the
fixed parameters and the hyperparameters of the random effects are
estimated by maximizing the marginal likelihood.

A group, a level of the grouping of a random term such as a patient or a
cell line, may own several conditions, which share the random effects of the
group.
Normal, spike-and-slab and Dirichlet-process random effects of one
grouping may be combined. Random terms over several groupings, nested or
crossed, are normal and integrated per connected component of the
conditions.

Methods:
\describe{
\item{\code{"focei"}}{Laplace approximation at the conditional modes with the
exact derivative of the volume term. The default, and the method for
several groupings; also for spike-and-slab and Dirichlet-process
random effects (at most 14 groups).}
\item{\code{"quadrature"}}{adaptive sparse-grid quadrature around the modes,
started from the FOCEI fit; section Quadrature. Also for spike-and-slab
random effects alone, per subset; section Sparse random effects.}
\item{\code{"laplaceEM"}}{ECM on the Laplace approximation with the
covariance-corrected update of \code{Omega}.}
\item{\code{"saem"}}{stochastic-approximation EM, also for spike-and-slab and
Dirichlet-process random effects; section SAEM. With many structural
parameters and sparse random effects it does not find the optimum from
random starts reliably; start it from a \code{"focei"} fit,
\code{em(obj, random, fit, method = "saem")}, to check that fit without the
Laplace approximation.}
}
}
\section{Quadrature}{

Method \code{"quadrature"} alternates between placing the nodes around the
modes at the current parameters and one trust step over every parameter on
these frozen nodes. The nodes of the mu-referenced fixed effects are frozen
in \verb{phi = X beta + eta}. Entries of \code{control$quadrature}:
\describe{
\item{\code{tol}}{allowed change of -2 log L by the next refinement, default
0.1, split over the groups. Each group starts from the Smolyak grid of
the stage and the grid of the last rebuild, and refines it
dimension-adaptively (Gerstner and Griebel 2003): the forward neighbors
of the grid are evaluated, their change of -2 log L is the error, and the
neighbor of largest change joins the grid until the error is within
\code{tol / G}. A grid therefore refines only the directions in which the
integrand departs from the Gaussian at the mode. Where the next
refinement exceeds \code{maxNodes}, the evaluated neighbors join the grid and
their change is the estimate. \code{Inf} keeps the grids of the stages.}
\item{\code{levels}}{the stages, default \code{K + 1} with \code{K} the number of random
effects of a group; level \code{K + s} integrates polynomials of total
degree \verb{2 s + 1} exactly.}
\item{\code{maxLevel}, \code{maxNodes}}{highest level, default \code{K + 8}, and the
largest number of evaluated nodes per group, default 1000; the level of
an adaptive grid is that of the smallest Smolyak grid holding it. A group whose
error exceeds \code{tol / G} at the highest level within both is capped.
Column \code{capped} of \code{stageTrace} counts the capped groups; attribute
\code{"levels"} of \code{stageTrace} and the \code{certificate} list level, level error
and cap per group.}
\item{\code{tolDecrement}}{a stage ends when a Newton step on the rebuilt
nodes would lower -2 log L by less than this, default 1e-4
(\code{stopReason} \code{"decrement"}), or when -2 log L rose after a step on nodes
of unchanged levels, so the step lies below what the levels resolve
(\code{"resolution"}). \code{converged} means stationary at the levels reached,
capped ones included; the fit returns the iterate of lowest -2 log L on
the highest levels.}
\item{\code{maxECMPerStage}, \code{cm1Control}, \code{maxCm1Iter}}{rebuilds per stage
(default 10, \code{stopReason} \code{"iterlim"} where they run out), \code{\link[=trust]{trust()}}
settings and iterations of a step (default 30).}
\item{\code{pruneTol}}{nodes whose weight lies this much in log below the
largest are dropped, default \code{Inf}.}
\item{\code{certificate}}{\code{FALSE}, or a list of \code{points} (default 256) and
\code{seed} (default 1): importance sampling at the estimate from a t5
proposal at the mode of each group. A group is flagged where the two
-2 log L differ by more than three standard errors plus the error the
level of the group allows, or the Pareto shape of the weights exceeds 0.7.}
\item{\code{epsECM}, \code{maxECM}}{for \code{"laplaceEM"}: the extrapolated distance to
the fixed point at which it stops, default 1e-5, and the iteration limit,
default 200.}
}
}

\section{SAEM}{

Metropolis draws of the random effects by random-walk and independence
proposals at the conditional modes, Robbins-Monro averages of their
sufficient statistics, the mu-referenced fixed effects and \code{Omega} in
closed form, the other parameters by Newton steps on averaged quadratic
models. Entries of \code{control$saem}:
\describe{
\item{\code{nBurnin}, \code{nEM}}{at most that many burn-in and averaging
iterations, default 200 and 300.}
\item{\code{nMcmc}, \code{nLaplace}}{random-walk and independence steps per
iteration, default 3 each; \code{nLaplace} with normal random effects alone.}
\item{\code{laplaceEvery}, \code{laplaceScale}}{iterations between refreshes of the
modes, default 5, with normal random effects alone, and the factor of the
proposal covariance, default 1.5, also of the slab value of an indicator
move.}
\item{\code{window}, \code{mcTol}}{the burn-in ends when no parameter has a
significant slope over the last \code{window} iterations (default 25), the
averaging when the expected rise of -2 log L from the Monte Carlo error
is below \code{mcTol} (default 0.05).}
\item{\code{particles}}{the particles of the sequential importance sampling
of -2 log L with Dirichlet-process random effects, default 500.}
\item{\code{cm1Control}}{\code{\link[=trust]{trust()}} settings of the Newton steps of the other
parameters, default one iteration per SAEM iteration.}
\item{\code{nFlip}}{with spike-and-slab random effects the sweeps over the
indicators per iteration, default 1.}
\item{\code{nodes}}{Gauss-Hermite nodes of the slab mass of an indicator, 3
(default) or 5.}
\item{\code{integral}}{\code{"importance"} takes -2 log L by importance sampling
over the partitions also where Dirichlet-process random effects beside
spike-and-slab ones allow the exact sum.}
}
With normal random effects alone, -2 log L at the estimate is the
quadrature of level \code{K + 2}, and \code{maxLevel}, \code{maxNodes} and \code{pruneTol} of
\code{control$quadrature} apply to it.
With spike-and-slab or Dirichlet-process random effects the averaging runs
\code{nEM} iterations. A sweep of Metropolis-Hastings moves visits every
indicator of a group once, in random order and for all groups at once,
with the slab value integrated out: the other slab values of the group
follow along the regression of the local quadratic model of the data, the
slab mass on that line is a Gauss-Hermite sum with the exact model at the
nodes, and the proposal draws the indicator from the resulting conditional
inclusion probability and the slab value from the local Gaussian; the
independence Metropolis-Hastings step keeps the posterior exact. \code{nMcmc}
random-walk steps move the slab values of every group jointly. \code{prob} and
\code{tau} follow in closed form from the averaged conditional inclusion
probabilities and slab second moments of the sweeps, not from the drawn
indicators; an estimated \code{tau} starts at least at the largest group mode
under the slab alone. An \code{em} fit as \code{init} starts the chain at the
selected random effects of that fit instead, the way to check a FOCEI
optimum by SAEM. The acceptance rates of births, deaths and random-walk
steps per iteration are columns of \code{stageTrace}. A Gibbs sweep draws
the clusters of the Dirichlet-process random effects (Neal 2000,
algorithm 8). The reported clusters are the most frequent partition of
the late draws, a spike-and-slab random effect is selected where the mean
conditional inclusion probability of the late sweeps is above 1/2, \code{p0}
one minus that mean. -2 log L is that of \code{method = "focei"} at the
estimate; with Dirichlet-process random effects alone, or beside
spike-and-slab ones on more than 14 groups, by importance sampling over the
partitions, with \code{valueSE}.
}

\section{Sparse random effects}{

With spike-and-slab random effects of the default \verb{spike = 0, mean = 0} or
Dirichlet-process random effects, \code{method = "focei"} moves the
parameters and hyperparameters in one trust region. The integral of a
group is exact where the data couple none of the factors and the sum over
the subsets of the spike-and-slab random effects of the group stays within a budget of
nodes; else it is the expectation consistent (EC) approximation, and a
trial point at which EC does not converge is a failed evaluation. Entries of \code{control$sparseFit}:
\describe{
\item{\code{maxOuter}}{iterations of the trust region, default 200.}
\item{\code{trustControl}}{the \code{\link[=trust]{trust()}} settings of the outer search. The modes
are found as by FOCEI, by the group kernel, where the Gauss-Newton step is
below 1e-8; of \code{control$innerControl} only \code{sqpIterlim} applies.}
\item{\code{burnin}, \code{phases}, \code{termBudget}, \code{ec}}{of the sum over subsets,
see below.}
}

Spike-and-slab random effects with \code{mean} or \code{spike} other than 0, or with
a hyperprior (\code{\link[=betaPrior]{betaPrior()}}), see \code{\link[=spikeSlab]{spikeSlab()}}, take a separate path of
\code{method = "focei"}, beside normal
random effects of the same grouping but no Dirichlet-process ones. The
integral of a group is the sum over all subsets of the spike-and-slab random
effects, each subset a Laplace integral over the normal random effects and
the slab ones of the subset at the inner mode, the other random effects at
the spike; \code{spike = -Inf} switches the
parameter off. The fixed effects and hyperparameters follow by EM:
\code{prob} is the mean inclusion probability, \code{tau^2} the mean squared deviation
from \code{mean} under the slab, and the fixed effects minimize the data term
plus slab prior summed over the subsets with their weights, jointly with
the subset modes; the log determinant of the Laplace integral enters the
weights only; Omega is the mean of the posterior second moments of the
normal random effects, restricted to the block structure. The \code{p0} column of
the random table is the posterior probability of the spike.

\code{method = "saem"} draws them by the moves of section SAEM: a spike at
\code{-Inf} leaves the other slab values as they are, and a move into the slab
proposes from the Laplace approximation of the coordinate given the others,
by Newton steps on the model. The reported -2 log L is the sum over subsets
of \code{method = "focei"} at the estimate; Dirichlet-process random effects
beside are refused.

\code{method = "quadrature"} takes the same path for every model with
spike-and-slab random effects, the default \code{spike = 0} included, after EM on
the Laplace integrals to their fixed point. Every subset of Laplace weight
above 1e-8 of the largest is integrated on a dimension-adaptive sparse grid
around the inner mode as in section Quadrature; a group has
\code{control$quadrature$tol / G} (default 0.1 / G), a subset of relative weight
\eqn{w} the share \eqn{1 / w} of it, so that light subsets stay on the first
grid; the other subsets keep the Laplace value. Then every parameter, the
Cholesky parameters of Omega and the estimated \code{prob} and \code{tau} included,
moves by trust steps on the frozen nodes, which are rebuilt after each step
until a Newton step would lower -2 log L by less than \code{tolDecrement}
(\code{stopReason} \code{"decrement"}). Of \code{control$quadrature} this path reads \code{tol},
\code{maxLevel} and \code{maxNodes} (per subset), \code{tolDecrement}, \code{maxECMPerStage},
\code{cm1Control} and \code{maxCm1Iter}.

\code{phases} lists the approximations of the sum over subsets in the order they
run, each from the estimates and inner modes of the phase before: a
character vector, or a named list whose entries set \code{maxECM}, the ECM
iterations (default 200), and \code{epsECM}, the largest change of an estimate
at which the phase ends (default 1e-2, in the last phase 1e-6). Each name
at most once; default \code{c("meanfield", "ec", "exact")}:
\describe{
\item{\code{"meanfield"}}{factorized inclusion per random effect, from the
Laplace integrals of a reference subset and of the subsets that differ
from it in one random effect; the reference is the subset of inclusion
above 1/2. A subset whose inner search fails has weight 0.}
\item{\code{"ec"}}{EC for every group, see below.}
\item{\code{"exact"}}{the sum over all subsets. Where the subsets summed over
the groups exceed \code{termBudget} (default 4096), the phase is skipped and
the fit ends with phase \code{"ec"}; without it, an error.}
}
\code{burnin} fits the fixed effects first, with \code{prob}, \code{tau} and \code{Omega} held,
in one trust region at the joint mode of a start subset of the
spike-and-slab random effects in the slab, the prior densities as penalty;
the others stay at the spike. \code{FALSE} (default) no burn-in, \code{TRUE} the
defaults, or a list of \code{start}, \code{"random"} (default, each in the slab with
probability \code{prob}, drawn from the random number stream), \code{"spike"}, \code{"slab"}
or the names of the random effects in the slab, and \code{iterlim}, the trust
iterations (default 200). The first phase starts from that subset. The fit
reports \code{burnin}, the start subset. \code{converged} is that of the last phase;
an error names the phase.

EC is the
expectation consistent approximation in a coordinate in which the spike is 0: the rate
\eqn{b^\eta}{b^eta} for \code{spike = -Inf}, where the slab is log-normal, and
\eqn{\eta}{eta} minus the spike else. The data term is a Gaussian in that
coordinate, expanded first at the mode with the random effects of inclusion
above 1/2 of the step before in the slab (at the first step every one), and
again at the EC mean until it is stable; normal random effects enter it
exactly, without sites. These groups take the subset of
inclusion probability above 1/2 for the fixed effects. Entries of
\code{control$sparseFit$ec}: \code{nodes} of the Gauss-Hermite rule of the log-normal
slab (40), \code{maxSweeps} (500) and \code{tolSweep} (1e-5, the change of the
marginal means in standard deviations), \code{maxRelinearize} (30) and
\code{tolRelinearize} (1e-4, relative).
}

\examples{
\donttest{
## Theophylline, 12 subjects: one-compartment model with first-order absorption in closed
## form, log parameters with a normal random intercept per subject, additive error
th <- datasets::Theoph
th$Subject <- as.character(th$Subject)
subjects <- unique(th$Subject)
data <- as.datalist(data.frame(name = "y", time = th$Time, value = th$conc, sigma = NA_real_,
                               condition = th$Subject))
## second derivatives forward over reverse, the default of em()
dm <- c("forward", "reverse", "forward-reverse")
x <- Xt()
g <- Y(c(y = "amt * Ka / (V * Ka - Cl) * (exp(-Cl / V * time) - exp(-Ka * time))"),
       states = "time", modelname = "theoph_obs", compile = FALSE, derivMode = dm,
       outdir = tempdir())
err <- Y(c(y = "sigma_add"), g, attachInput = FALSE, modelname = "theoph_err", compile = FALSE,
         derivMode = dm, outdir = tempdir())
## one condition per subject with the subject dose; the effect formula replaces each log parameter
## by an intercept plus a random effect per subject
doses <- tapply(th$Dose * th$Wt, th$Subject, `[`, 1)[subjects]
trafo <- branch(define(NULL, "x <- x", x = c("Ka", "V", "Cl", "amt", "sigma_add")),
                table = data.frame(ID = subjects, dose = doses, row.names = subjects))
trafo <- insert(trafo, "amt <- dose", dose = dose)
trafo <- insert(trafo, "x <- exp(x)", x = c("Ka", "V", "Cl", "sigma_add"))
p <- P(insert(trafo, Ka + V + Cl ~ 1 + (1 | ID)), method = "explicit", compile = FALSE,
       modelname = "theoph_p", derivMode = dm, outdir = tempdir())
compile(g, err, p, output = tempfile("theoph"))
obj <- normL2(data, g * x * p, errmodel = err)
init <- c(Ka = 0, V = 3, Cl = 0.7, sigma_add = log(0.5))

## FOCEI fit, the estimates and the random effects per subject
fit <- em(obj, randomEffects(p), init = init)
fit
summary(fit)
fit$omega
head(fit$random)

## quadrature started from the FOCEI estimates, and SAEM
fitQ <- em(obj, randomEffects(p), init = fit, method = "quadrature", verbose = FALSE)
fitS <- em(obj, randomEffects(p), init = init, method = "saem", verbose = FALSE)
c(focei = fit$value, quadrature = fitQ$value, saem = fitS$value)
}
\dontrun{
## Ka and Cl with correlated random effects, V with a spike-and-slab one: a second transformation
p2 <- P(insert(trafo, Ka + Cl ~ 1 + (1 | p | ID), V ~ 1 + (1 | gr(ID, dist = spikeSlab()))),
        method = "explicit", compile = FALSE, modelname = "theoph_p2", derivMode = dm,
        outdir = tempdir())
compile(p2, output = tempfile("theoph"))
fit2 <- em(normL2(data, g * x * p2, errmodel = err), randomEffects(p2), init = init)
fit2$omega
fit2$slab
AIC(fit, fit2)
}
}
